<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Halberstadt</h1><p class="st-meta">1648 CE – 1807 CE · capital Halberstadt</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Former prince-bishopric in the Harz foreland turned into a Brandenburg-Prussian principality by the Peace of Westphalia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a>, founded around 804, had become Lutheran during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and was ruled by administrators. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 it was secularised and given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> as compensation for its claims to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Pomerania</a>, becoming the Principality of Halberstadt.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was governed by Brandenburg-Prussian officials through a provincial government, with the cathedral chapter kept as a Protestant institution. It was a fertile grain-growing region with a sizeable garrison. In the 18th century the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wilhelm-ludwig-gleim">Johann Wilhelm Ludwig Gleim</a> lived there as cathedral secretary, gathering a circle of writers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia's</a> defeat in 1806, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a> was given to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-westphalia">Kingdom of Westphalia</a> in 1807. It returned to Prussia in 1815 and became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-saxony">Province of Saxony</a>; the town is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1648 CE – 1807 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.50%;width:3.16%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1648 CE – 1807 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Halberstadt</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick William, the Great Elector, Frederick II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fürstentum Halberstadt</dd></dl></aside></div>
